// ===========================================================================
// engine/system/release_fault.emp — the LEAN-shape loud-failure handler
// ===========================================================================
// The LEAN shape (`sigil build --native --lean`, CRASH_REPORT=0) is the one
// build that ships NO MD Debugger / error_handler island — but a fault must
// still HALT LOUDLY rather than corrupt silently. ReleaseFault is that loud
// failure: mask every interrupt, reset the VDP command state machine, set the
// backdrop RED, and freeze. It is the LEAN arm of every fault vector
// ($08-$2C class stubs, $30-$60 reserved/spurious, $64/$68/$6C/$74/$7C IRQ,
// $80-$FC TRAP) — vectors.emp points all 60 at this proc when CRASH_REPORT=0,
// exactly where the debug + release shapes point them at error_handler's
// per-class stubs. The owner ruling (vectors.emp policy block, item 27)
// requires that any shape without the debugger PRESERVE loud failure; this is
// that implementation. (The name says RELEASE and the shape that carries it is
// LEAN: under the 2026-08-04 crash-report ruling the release shape carries the
// debugger, so lean is the debugger-less shape this handler serves.)
//
// SEMANTICS — ruled, implement exactly (do NOT "improve"):
//   * NO stack use — a bus/address fault may have landed with a wild SSP; every
//     write here is register/absolute, never a push.
//   * NO rte — a group-0 fault (bus/address error) pushes a 14-byte exception
//     frame the 68000's `rte` MISPARSES: it pops the function-code word as SR
//     and the access-address longword as PC, so `rte` from such a frame runs off
//     into garbage. `rte` is unusable here.
//   * NO rts — nothing called us with a return address to trust.
//   * NO Z80-bus hold — the bus-request busy-wait could hang forever (before
//     the red screen ever paints) if the Z80 is wedged; every VDP write below is
//     best-effort, so we never gate the red screen on the sound CPU.
//   * A double bus fault while the CPU pushes its OWN exception frame halts the
//     68000 outright — which is ALSO acceptably loud (a frozen machine), so the
//     no-stack posture cannot make the failure quieter.
// The d0 clobber (VDP status readback) and the sr clobber (the terminal mask)
// are both irrelevant in practice: this proc is TERMINAL, it never returns, so
// no caller observes any register or the interrupt mask. They are declared
// because the contract is a statement about the BODY.
//
// It deliberately does NOT live in vectors.emp (that file is the fixed org-0
// image, pure data to $200); it sits near the ROM tail in map order, at the same
// tail position the error_handler island takes in the shapes that carry it.
// LEAN-shape ONLY: the registry pushes it under the `else` of
// `if debug || crash_report`, so neither canonical shape carries it. It is
// therefore absent from every canonical listing, which is why it has no `repin`
// region — lean places it from its frozen size table like every other
// off-canonical section.
// ===========================================================================

module engine.system.release_fault in release_fault

use engine.constants.{VDP_CTRL, VDP_DATA}
use engine.vdp.{vdp_comm, VdpTarget, VdpOp}

section release_fault (cpu: m68000) {
    // Terminal fault handler — a hardware/vector entry that never returns (see
    // the clobber note above). `sr` is a clobber and never a preserve: the mask
    // is permanent by design, so nothing restores it.
    pub proc ReleaseFault () clobbers(d0/sr) {
        move.w  #$2700, sr                  // terminal: mask every interrupt level
        move.w  VDP_CTRL, d0                // read: reset the VDP command state
                                            // machine (the fault may have landed
                                            // mid-command; this re-primes it)
        move.w  #$8134, VDP_CTRL            // reg 1 = display OFF (M5+DMA kept,
                                            // VBlank-int moot under the SR mask).
                                            // With the display off the ENTIRE
                                            // raster is the backdrop colour —
                                            // without this, a scene whose planes
                                            // cover the screen hides the red
                                            // completely (oracle-proven on OJZ:
                                            // the freeze looked like a normal
                                            // frame until this write was added)
        move.w  #$8700, VDP_CTRL            // reg 7 = backdrop is palette line 0,
                                            // colour index 0 → backdrop = CRAM[0]
        move.l  #vdp_comm(0, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL  // point at CRAM[0]
        move.w  #$000E, VDP_DATA            // CRAM[0] = red (Genesis BGR: $000E)
    .halt:
        bra     .halt                       // frozen; state preserved for the
                                            // emulator post-mortem
    }
}
